a generative model."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "no", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "EC3_not_generative", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes a pre-trained transformer for single-cell data using cells as tokens and tissues as sentences, but does not mention any generative mechanism (decoder, autoregressive, diffusion) or generation of sequences; it focuses on encoding cell-cell relations for downstream tasks, thus not a generative model.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_needs_adjudication": false}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/prompt_iteration_runs/prompt_iter2_wrapper_relax_highsignal25_deepseekv4_3gpu_11472_20260509T131343Z/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:2:{"cluster_id": "benchmark:3", "title": "EpiAgent: foundation model for single-cell epigenomics", "abstract": "Abstract\n        Large-scale foundation models have recently opened a new avenue to artificial general intelligence for life sciences, showing great promise in the analysis of single-cell transcriptomic data. Nevertheless, such challenges as the tremendous number of signaling regions, extreme data sparsity, and the nearly binary nature of single-cell epigenomic data have prevented the construction of a foundation model for epigenomics thus far, though it is evident that abundant epigenomic properties such as chromatin accessibility provide more decisive insights into cell states than transcriptomics, shaping the chromatin regulatory landscapes that control transcription in distinct cell types. Here, we introduce EpiAgent, the first foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data, pretrained on a manually curated large-scale Human-scATAC-Corpus that is comprised of approximately 5 million cells and 35 billion tokens. EpiAgent encodes chromatin accessibility patterns of cells as concise “cell sentences,” and employs a bidirectional attention mechanism to capture cellular heterogeneity behind regulatory networks. With comprehensive benchmarks, we demonstrate that EpiAgent excels in typical downstream tasks, including unsupervised feature extraction, supervised cell type annotation, and data imputation. By incorporating external embeddings, EpiAgent facilitates the prediction of cellular responses to both out-of-sample stimulated and unseen genetic perturbations, as well as reference data integration and query data mapping. By simulating the knockout of key cis-regulatory elements, EpiAgent enables in-silico treatment for cancer analysis. We further extended zero-shot capabilities of EpiAgent, allowing direct cell type annotation on newly sequenced datasets without additional training.", "regression_group": "benchmark_boundary_case", "decision_run_a": NaN, "decision_run_b": NaN, "scope_boundary_run_a": NaN, "scope_boundary_run_b": NaN, "rationale_run_a": NaN, "rationale_run_b": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 3, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "EC2_no_text_component", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "none", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "biological_token_only_boundary", "decision_rationale": "EpiAgent uses 'cell sentences' which are biological tokens, not natural-language text. No text component is present, so it does not meet the text criterion for generative text-bio models."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "EC2_no_text_component", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "biological_token_only_boundary", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "EpiAgent uses 'cell sentences' which are biological tokens, not natural-language text. No text component is present, so it does not meet the text criterion for generative text-bio models.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "generative_model_present": "no", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "EC3_not_generative", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "decision_rationale": "EpiAgent uses a bidirectional attention mechanism (encoder-only) for single-cell epigenomic data; no generative mechanism described. Therefore, generative_model_present=no."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "no", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "EC3_not_generative", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "EpiAgent uses a bidirectional attention mechanism (encoder-only) for single-cell epigenomic data; no generative mechanism described. Therefore, generative_model_present=no.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_needs_adjudication": false}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/prompt_iteration_runs/prompt_iter2_wrapper_relax_highsignal25_deepseekv4_3gpu_11472_20260509T131343Z/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:3:{"cluster_id": "benchmark:5", "title": "GeneGPT: Augmenting Large Language Models with Domain Tools for Improved Access to Biomedical Information", "abstract": "While large language models (LLMs) have been successfully applied to various tasks, they still face challenges with hallucinations. Augmenting LLMs with domain-specific tools such as database utilities can facilitate easier and more precise access to specialized knowledge. In this paper, we present GeneGPT, a novel method for teaching LLMs to use the Web APIs of the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) for answering genomics questions. Specifically, we prompt Codex to solve the GeneTuring tests with NCBI Web APIs by in-context learning and an augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls. Experimental results show that GeneGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance on eight tasks in the GeneTuring benchmark with an average score of 0.83, largely surpassing retrieval-augmented LLMs such as the new Bing (0.44), biomedical LLMs such as BioMedLM (0.08) and BioGPT (0.04), as well as GPT-3 (0.16) and ChatGPT (0.12). Our further analyses suggest that: (1) API demonstrations have good cross-task generalizability and are more useful than documentations for in-context learning; (2) GeneGPT can generalize to longer chains of API calls and answer multi-hop questions in GeneHop, a novel dataset introduced in this work; (3) Different types of errors are enriched in different tasks, providing valuable insights for future improvements.", "regression_group": "benchmark_boundary_case", "decision_run_a": NaN, "decision_run_b": NaN, "scope_boundary_run_a": NaN, "scope_boundary_run_b": NaN, "rationale_run_a": NaN, "rationale_run_b": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 4, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"pa