./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/prompt_iteration_runs/prompt_iter2_wrapper_relax_highsignal25_deepseekv4_3gpu_11472_20260509T131343Z/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:24:{"cluster_id": "2652", "title": "Generative single-cell transcriptomics via large language models", "abstract": "ABSTRACT\n                \n                  Single-cell and spatial transcriptomics have generated vast atlases of cellular states, yet these data are almost exclusively used for analysis rather than generation. Here we introduce the LLM-based model PGL, Portraying Gene Language, a framework that reframes single-cell transcriptomes as a language generation modeling problem. PGL represents each cell as a long sequence of gene–expression tokens and uses a large language model to synthesize complete single-cell RNA-seq profiles from metadata alone, such as tissue and disease context. PGL-generated cells recapitulate dataset-specific transcriptomic structure, align with known cancer subtype biology, and mix coherently with real single-cell datasets. Notably, generated cells can be used as effective references for spatial transcriptomics, enabling accurate cell-type mapping without matched single-cell atlases. By shifting single-cell modeling from representation learning to cell generation, PGL enables virtual cohort construction, hypothesis generation, and reference-on-demand analysis, positioning generative language models as foundational tools for\n                  in silico\n                  single-cell biology.", "regression_group": "include_decision_unstable", "decision_run_a": "INCLUDE", "decision_run_b": "EXCLUDE", "scope_boundary_run_a": NaN, "scope_boundary_run_b": NaN, "rationale_run_a": "PGL is a generative model that uses text metadata (tissue, disease) to synthesize single-cell transcriptomes, representing a primary text-bio model. | The abstract introduces PGL, a generative LLM-based model for synthesizing single-cell transcriptomes from metadata, clearly indicating a generative architecture and broad applicability as a foundational tool.", "rationale_run_b": "The model uses gene-expression tokens and an LLM to generate single-cell profiles, but no natural-language text component is described; biological tokenization alone does not count as a text component. | The abstract introduces PGL, an LLM-based generative model that synthesizes single-cell RNA-seq profiles from metadata, clearly indicating a generative architecture. It demonstrates broad applicability (e.g., spatial transcriptomics reference, virtual cohorts) and transferable representations, satisfying foundation-model evidence.", "_pilot_row_id": 23, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "EC2_no_text_component", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "none", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "biological_token_only_boundary", "decision_rationale": "The model uses gene-expression tokens and categorical metadata (tissue, disease) but no natural-language text component, thus failing the text component criterion."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "EC2_no_text_component", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "biological_token_only_boundary", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The model uses gene-expression t